Eventually this becomes a served page. For now it is a working document.


================================================================================
WHO A TESTER IS
================================================================================

A TESTER IS NOT A ROLE. There is no "tester" account type and no permission
for it. A tester is simply someone with a login and no extra privileges — the
plain `user` role.

That is deliberate. Everyone who has a login can be a tester: developers and
administrators too. Downloading a build, leaving a comment or reporting a
problem are things any signed-in person can do, not a special grant.

So being invited "as a tester" just means being invited as a plain user. You
get no workspace and no upload rights, and none of that is a restriction being
placed on you — there is simply nothing else a login carries by default.


================================================================================
GETTING IN
================================================================================

Someone with admin rights invites you by email address. You receive a link:

    /register?token=...

Open it and choose a username and password.

The link is valid for 28 days. If it expires, the same page offers to generate
a new one, up to six times before an administrator must issue a fresh
invitation.

If your invitation was withdrawn, or has already been used, the page says so
rather than showing you a form that cannot work.

The invitation is emailed to you, if the portal has been configured to send
mail. If it has not, whoever invited you is given the link to pass on
themselves, so a link still reaches you either way.

Two-factor authentication is required at login. You will be asked for a code
from an authenticator app.

WHAT YOUR ACCOUNT CAN DO

  * Sign in, and see your own landing page.
  * Follow the Discussion link to the team's board.

That is genuinely all. There is no build to download yet and no way to report
anything yet — see below.


================================================================================
WHAT A TESTER WILL BE ABLE TO DO
================================================================================

NOT BUILT — none of the following exists yet. This is the intended shape.

  * Download a build of the game, and older versions of it.
  * Leave comments, and read other people's.
  * Report bugs, and follow up on ones you have reported.
  * Talk to the team. See the Discussion section below.

The intent is that a tester never needs a copy of the source, or any tooling.
Download, play, say what you found.


================================================================================
DISCUSSION
================================================================================

The team talks on a board separate from this portal. It is NOT built into the
portal and will not be, and that is a decision rather than an oversight: the
board is being chosen to be open source and hosted in the UK or the EU, so the
conversation is not subject to US jurisdiction.

WHAT THAT MEANS FOR YOU, and it is worth knowing before you sign up:

  * YOUR BOARD ACCOUNT IS SEPARATE from your portal account. Signing in here
    does not sign you in there, and your role here does not carry over.
  * Signing out here does not sign you out of the board.
  * Revoking your portal access will revoke your board access too — that is
    being built deliberately, because it is the thing a third-party board
    normally cannot do.

NOT BUILT YET. The board has not been chosen and configured. The Discussion
link on the portal says so rather than offering a link that goes nowhere, which
is the correct behaviour and not an error.

The page is deliberately honest about that rather than describing features that
are coming as though they were here.


================================================================================
WHAT A TESTER CANNOT DO
================================================================================

  * See inside a developer's workspace. It is private to them, and to nobody
    else — including administrators, except by impersonation, which is marked
    and audited rather than routine.
  * Upload or change assets.
  * Approve anything.

None of that is a technicality to work around. The workspace is private because
developers need to be able to make a mess while they work, and that only works
if nobody is watching over their shoulder.


================================================================================
REVOCATION
================================================================================

Your invitation can be withdrawn before you use it, and your ACCESS can be
revoked after — by deactivating the account.

That takes effect IMMEDIATELY. If you are signed in when it happens, your very
next action is refused — you are not left working until your session happens to
expire. Being deactivated does not delete anything you have done.

An administrator can reactivate you, which restores access immediately in the
same way. If you think a deactivation was a mistake, contact whoever invited
you; there is no self-service route back in.


